---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import TimezoneConverter from '@/components/tools/datetime/TimezoneConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="timezone-converter">
  <TimezoneConverter client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a time between zones</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the date and time, and the time zone of that time.</li>
      <li>Add the zones you want to compare. This device remembers your selection for next time.</li>
      <li>Read the table: each row is the same instant, written as a person in that zone would write it.</li>
      <li>
        Use the overlap strip to find an hour that suits everyone, then tap a column to set the reference time to
        that hour.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Reading the overlap strip</h2>
    <p>
      Each row is one time zone and each of the 24 columns is one hour of the reference day. The number in a cell
      is the local hour in that zone. Green cells are inside the working window you set, amber cells are waking
      hours outside it, and grey cells are night. A dot above a column means every selected zone is in working
      hours at that hour, so that column is a possible meeting slot.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rows can be shifted by a day, and that is correct: 09:00 Monday in San Francisco is 02:00 Tuesday in
      Berlin. The date column and the "next day" note show the shift, so nobody books a meeting on the wrong
      day.
    </p>

    <h2>Daylight saving time</h2>
    <p>
      Offsets change during the year. About 70 countries change their clocks, on different days. Most scheduling
      errors happen in the gaps between those days:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Mid-March: the US moves its clocks forward two to three weeks before Europe, so New York and London are 4
        hours apart, not the usual 5, for that period.
      </li>
      <li>
        Late October: Europe moves its clocks back a week before the US, so the gap is 4 hours again for a week.
      </li>
      <li>
        Southern hemisphere: Australia and New Zealand change in the opposite direction, so the gap between Europe
        and Sydney changes by two hours over the year.
      </li>
      <li>
        No DST: most of Asia, Africa and South America, and Arizona, Hawaii, Queensland and Saskatchewan.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The time zone converter uses your browser's IANA time zone database, which has the rules for each zone,
      including historical changes. So it applies the offset in force on the date you selected, not today's.
      Change the date across a transition and the offsets change.
    </p>

    <h2>Why zone names beat abbreviations</h2>
    <p>
      Abbreviations are ambiguous; do not use them to schedule. CST is Central Standard Time in North America,
      China Standard Time and Cuba Standard Time. IST is India, Ireland and Israel. BST is British Summer Time and
      Bangladesh Standard Time. Even unambiguous ones change between standard and summer forms twice a year, so
      "EST" written in June is usually wrong: the sender meant EDT.
    </p>
    <p>
      IANA identifiers such as Europe/Lisbon or America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires name a region with a shared history
      of clock changes, so software uses them. The pickers here list these identifiers.
    </p>

    <h2>Meeting planning across time zones</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Give the instant, not the wall clock. "14:00 UTC" or "14:00 Europe/Berlin" is unambiguous; "2pm my time"
        is not.
      </li>
      <li>
        Watch the date line. A meeting across Asia-Pacific and the Americas falls on different calendar days for
        different people.
      </li>
      <li>
        Share the inconvenience. With a wide spread, no hour suits everybody. Alternate which region takes the
        early or late call; do not always favour headquarters.
      </li>
      <li>
        Avoid the transition weeks. A recurring meeting near the edge of the working day moves outside it in March
        and October for half the participants.
      </li>
      <li>
        Nine-hour rule of thumb: Europe and the US west coast, or Europe and East Asia, have about one comfortable
        overlap hour. Plan for it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Half-hour and quarter-hour offsets</h2>
    <p>
      Some zones are not a whole number of hours from UTC. India is UTC+05:30, Iran UTC+03:30, Newfoundland
      UTC−03:30, Nepal UTC+05:45 and the Chatham Islands UTC+12:45. The difference column shows fractional hours,
      and the overlap strip puts those zones on the nearest hour. Check the exact time in the table before you
      send an invite.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
